An environmental expert is questioning the methodology used for tests to measure lead levels in children living near the Port Pirie smelter in South Australia.
The latest round of health screening in Port Pirie, an industrial city 220 kilometres north of Adelaide, shows the level of lead found in toddlers is rising.
There is a lead smelter in the town owned by Nyrstar.
The figures show the average two-year-old child has 6.3 micrograms of lead per decilitre of blood, a slightly higher level than the 6.1 microgram average 12 months ago.
The World Health Organisation says a safe level is anywhere below 10.
But Mark Taylor, professor of environmental science at Macquarie University in Sydney, has questioned the data being used in the analysis, saying levels could be even higher.
"If somebody turns up with a blood lead level of 20 micrograms per decilitre in the early part of the year, for example, that child may undergo some intervention at the home; the parents will be given some educational advice and [there] maybe some clean-up at that house," he said.
"Over time that blood level may fall down to 8 or 9 micrograms per decilitre... [which is what] goes into the final calculation.
"That down-presses the average and the mean result."
Professor Taylor says that methodology could be "easily interpreted" as a manipulation of the results.
"To some people it would look like that," he said.
"It would be better if they were more upfront with the information of reducing the average level of exposure in children."
